// CASE: The Empty Tomb
// CASE ID: roman_inquiry
// CASE EXPORT: resurrection
// SOURCE: act4_case_2d.js → NPC 'mary_magdalene' (dialogueId: mary_resurrection)
// BIBLE REFERENCE: John 20:1–18; Mark 16:9–11
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPHECIES FULFILLED IN THIS CASE:
//   - Isaiah 53:10–11
//     Gospel: John 20:11–18 — After suffering, the Suffering Servant lives
//     Insight: Isaiah 53:10 prophesied that the righteous servant would be resurrected after suffering — 'After' (Hebrew: 'acharit') implies sequential timing. The resurrection is the vindication of the crucifixion.
//   - Psalm 16:10
//     Gospel: Acts 2:27–31 — David's psalm applied to Jesus's resurrection
//     Insight: David wrote in first person of not seeing corruption — Peter applied this to Jesus's resurrection on the third day, arguing the Psalm was prophetic about the Messiah.
//   - Jonah 1:17 / Matthew 12:40
//     Gospel: Matthew 12:40 — Jesus predicted His three-day burial and resurrection
//     Insight: Jesus explicitly connected His death and resurrection to Jonah's three days in the fish — both involve being 'cast into the deep' and emerging to complete a divine mission.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// BIBLICAL CONTEXT:
//   Summary: Early on Sunday morning, Nisan 17, Mary Magdalene came to the garden tomb and found the stone rolled away. She saw the empty tomb and initially mistook the risen Christ for a gardener.
//   Insight: The fact that Mary, a former demoniac and low-status woman, was the first witness to the resurrection is powerful evidence of authenticity — no fabricator would choose her as the primary witness.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
-> start
=== start ===
I came to the garden while it was still dark. I expected to find a sealed tomb and a heavy heart. Instead, I found the world had changed.
* [The stone was already moved?] -> stone
* [Did you look inside?] -> inside
=== stone ===
Yes. Rolled away like a pebble. I ran for the others, certain that someone had stolen the body. How could the tomb be empty?
-> closing
=== inside ===
Later, I looked in and saw the linens. They weren't torn or scattered. They were just... there. Empty.
He had passed through them, leaving death behind like an old cloak.
-> closing
=== closing ===
I didn't understand then that life doesn't need to steal what it already owns. The Gardener called my name — 'Mary.' And I knew.
-> DONE